/-- p25022 — 25:E4. A single hard sentence, system-relatively: the bare evaluation calculus infers $\forall x\,\varphi(x)$ from the $m$ premises $\varphi(c)$. Every derivation of a global clause there has length at least $m$ and defeats every budget $H<m$. A richer $\D$ may derive some global clauses briefly. -/
theorem p25022 : (∀ (m : Nat) (l : List FRC.Reach.Line), FRC.Reach.RuleApplies m l → m ≤ l.length) ∧ (∀ (m : Nat) (l : List FRC.Reach.Line), FRC.Reach.RuleApplies m l → ∀ (H : Nat), H < m → ¬l.length + (1 : Nat) ≤ H) ∧ ∀ (M : FRC.Logic.FinStr) (env : Nat → Nat) (φ : FRC.Logic.FForm), (∀ (c : Nat), c < M.m → FRC.Logic.fval M (FRC.Logic.cons c env) φ = true) → FRC.Logic.Sat M env φ.all :=
